Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A good conception of justice is one which accommodates our intuitions (our \"firmest convictions\") about morality and also derives a moral structure above that which can be used to arrive at conclusions about more controversial moral topics where intuition fails.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is criteria by which Rawls judges his own conception of justice (justice as fairness).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A good conception of justice should be able to allow for the moral convictions that we intuitively hold to be true, but at the same time, provide a structure that is able to ensure that other moral issues which are not easily addressed through intuition are likewise covered under the moral system.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons\">Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is the reason why Justice as fairness would so easily be accepted? Because it fits our intuitions, but also offers a robust structure for dealing with more controversial moral topics.</li></ul></li></ul>","Acting virtuously is not painful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle says that acting virtuously should not be painful (so it should at least be painless), and in many cases, it would actually be pleasurable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an interesting part of Aristotle philosophy because it seems contradictory, as he names some virtuous that are painful.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that the concept of “pain” is somewhat complicated in the case of Aristotle’s ethics. Although not formally stated, there is a rough bifurcation possible when it comes to examining what he means by “pain.”</p><ol><li>Physical pain: as the name suggests.</li><li>Psychological pain: negative mental states, such as distress, sorrow, grief, fear, shame, etc. </li></ol><p>When Aristotle states that virtues are not painful, he is approximately referring to the psychological definition of pain.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>By extension, it means that acting in the mean is not painful.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal\">The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal</a><ul><li>Inferentially, the process of attaining a goal of acting moral would not be painful.</li></ul></li></ul>","Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In scientific research, the use of agentic AI has the potential to radically speed up the rate of scientific research, leading to more breakthroughs in far less time. AI does not only need to be used as a tool for merely analyzing data.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This use of AI could potentially lead to radical development of a huge amount of scientific innovations that could have huge upside for humanity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>AI would be especially useful for fields with high marginal returns to intelligence, since this is the area in which AI excels. What’s more, once the supply of researchers increases, the marginal cost of research decreases, meaning that agents will be able to pursue research into research with ostensibly low expected value, which could have high potential upsides.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a><ul><li>Perhaps AI research could solve this problem? They could potentially be able to coordinate research better.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)\">The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)</a><ul><li>AI researchers could also potentially mitigate this?</li></ul></li></ul>","As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the modernization thesis, associated with liberal political theory which states that nations generally converge on liberal democracy when they become more prosperous. This thesis has been near conclusively disproven.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was a major hypothesis for a while in political science and international relations, though its since been rejected since there are powerful counterexamples.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Examples that go against this thesis include:</p><ul><li>China</li><li>Singapore</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade brings people into contact, not agreement\">Trade brings people into contact, not agreement</a><ul><li>Trade, which tends to enrich the people, doesn’t necessarily bring them into agreement, just contact.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability\">Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability</a><ul><li>The opposite of the previous claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>It seems that this note is evidence against this claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a><ul><li>Generally, economic freedom is required for people to become rich. However, it seems like there are cases where even though economic freedom has been achieved, political freedom has not.</li></ul></li></ul>","Attaching ancillary requirements to a project prevents it from delivering its core objective":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When every public project is required to serve procurement rules, labour standards, environmental review and local consultation at once, the accumulated conditions make the primary objective unreachable, so the programme delivers none of its aims rather than most of them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This identifies a failure mode that does not show up in any individual decision, since each requirement is defensible on its own and only the stack is fatal.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Klein advances it as a case for supply-side progressivism, which keeps the ends of the political left and abandons the practice of loading each instrument with the whole programme.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>Why the requirements cannot be relaxed case by case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people&#x27;s misunderstanding of what causes the issue\">The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people's misunderstanding of what causes the issue</a><ul><li>The clearest domain in which this occurs.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>The general pattern of which this is an instance.</li></ul></li></ul>","Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Baumol’s Cost Disease, which is when high productivity sectors force a rise in wages of lower productivity sectors, can slow economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the main factors that when new innovations appear which can galvanize the economy, they impact is often muted, as costs are rising across the board.</p><h4>Effects</h4><p>For example, labor will shift from highly progressive (high increases in labor productivity) sectors to stagnant sectors (low increases in labor productivity).  The Baumol Effect has a large effect on sectors such as the performance arts, healthcare, or education, where there isn’t a huge amount is innovation/little efficiency gains. It seems that the effect of this wage equalization would over power the depressed wages that would otherwise result from the influx of labor into low-productivity sectors, since the overall effect is one of wage increase (despite the increased labor leaving high-productivity sectors for low-productivity sectors.)</p><ul><li>I suppose this is an empirical question, since the reason that low productivity sectors would raise wages is due to the fact that workers would otherwise leave for sectors with higher wages, but since there is an influx of new workers, it seems that this effect would be weakened substantially, if not reversed. </li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth\">Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth\">A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth</a></li></ul>","Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Compensating differentials is the idea that some jobs pay more due to the non-pecuniary benefits (or disadvantages) associated with employment. This is evident for example for lighthouse workers, who receive very high pay as they are isolated and the job is also dangerous.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can explain some of the variation in the job market, and why some jobs pay a lot more, despite being less technical or physical demanding than some other jobs.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society, though it is a factor that can influence political development.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a critique levied at Samuel Huntington’s idea of Cultural Determinism, that culture is that will underly all political differences between states after the end of the Cold War.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The same culture that lead to a progressive parliamentarian democracy in the Netherlands led to Apartheid in South Africa.</li><li>Korea, due to its Asian culture, was thought to be despotic in nature.<ul><li>This idea that Asian states are naturally despotic have even been used by Asian dictators to legitimize their rule.</li></ul></li><li>Turkey, due to its Islamic culture, ought to resemble the other Middle Eastern countries, rather than adopting Western legal and political institutions.<ul><li>I think that this is debatable through, since Turkey has long had a much greater exposure to the West and it’s institutions.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a><ul><li>A relevant distinction that needs to be made in order for this point to stand.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</span><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>This could potentially be used as a point to support this argument, since the vastly different cultures of China and India lead to vastly different institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a><ul><li>Again, this would be another point that supports the idea of cultural determinism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Same as above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a><ul><li>Modernization theory is something that, although debated in and of itself, refutes the idea of Cultural Determinism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India\">Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India</a><ul><li>An example of Culture’s impact on institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>Another example.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>A related idea of Geographical Determinism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development\">Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development</a><ul><li>An argument against this idea; a general rejection of Cultural Determinism for a more general idea of conflict being the result of opposing ideologies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Divine providence is nothing but the collective instinct of mankind":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That the idea of providence in human history has been nothing but a representation of the collective instincts of humans.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was proposed by Proudhon as a means of secularizing the concept of providence and providing a secular explanation for the reason of its being.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>History thus does not advance as a result of providence. Rather, it is concrete human progress (replacing antiquated notions and cultural mores with updated ones) that represents true historical progress. In this way, we have to reject the concept of divine providence.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p.63</p><p>Far from being directed by providential destiny, history advances by revolutionary crises that give birth to new conceptions of justice. The first crisis was provoked by Jesus when he proclaimed man's equality before God. The second was inaugurated by the Reformation and Descartes, achieving equality before conscience and reason. The third began with the French Revolution and established equality before the law. The coming revolution, which is economic and social, will mark the end of the religious, aristocratic, and bourgeois age. It will bring about final equality by the \"equation of man with humanity.\" <strong>To effect this ultimate advance, man has to take up the eternal fight between man and God and decide it; for God, or the Absolute, is the one great obstacle to human progress and the one great source of all kinds of absolutism-economic, political, religious.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>Note</h4><p>Yet, the very idea of achieving something greater, the idea that the universe even has a <em>telos</em> at all, is something that is, by nature, Christian. Therefore, Proudhon, though he blasphemed and attacked Christianity, is attempting to merely create an alternative for the eschatological niche carved originally by Christianity. He merely argues for the “defatalization” of the Christian doctrine of providence, not its abolition.</p><h4>References</h4>","Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Friendship that is based on utility or pleasure are easily dissolved, since these things can be fleeting, and once they are gone, there is no reason for these friendships to continue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial factor that separates friendship based on utility or pleasure from those based on virtue.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><strong>Utility</strong>: A friendship based on a business relationship may end after a deal is reached.<ul><li>These friendships are often formed between people who are opposites, since they each have something the other does not (e.g., rich and poor, dumb and smart).</li><li>In this type of friendship, the value of a service is determined by the utility conferred to the recipient of a service (not by the conferrer of the service).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Pleasure</strong>: A friendship based a mutual enjoyment of 18th-century furniture may end because of the participants find a new interest.</li></ul><p>They are easily dissolved since in these types of friendships, people only care about their own utility or pleasure, and not anything about the other person. So the moment they stop gaining anything from the relationship, they end it.</p><ul><li>Most quarrels between friends occur because the individuals are mistaken about the basis of their friendship (i.e., one persons believes the friendship to be one of utility, and the other, pleasure).</li></ul><p>Truly good people do not make friendships of the kinds outlined in this note, only friendships of the virtuous kind.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>A friendship based on moral virtue, unlike what is described here, lasts longer.</li></ul></li></ul>","Hamlet (play)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of Shakespeare's four great tragedies. Considered by many to be Shakespeare's best, and the best play ever written.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is required reading for AP Lit. But also, I have always been interested in reading Shakespeare's work.</p><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Literature can dramatize metacognition by depicting characters reasoning about their own reasoning\">Literature can dramatize metacognition by depicting characters reasoning about their own reasoning</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work\">An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Oedipus complex appears in many works of literature\">The Oedipus complex appears in many works of literature</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content\">Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed\">The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>There are many similarities between these two plays.</li></ul></li></ul>","Highland Light (painting)":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>The way that the frame is slightly tilted creates a sense of incoherence and confusion for the viewer, and also, as one observes closely, the lighthouse is way to big for the background of the view, which means that the perspective would only make sense if the lighthouse were placed at the bottom of a cliff such that only the top of the lighthouse shows. These inconsistencies create a sense of the disquiet in the viewer.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cat-walk (painting)\">Cat-walk (painting)</a><ul><li>See the above painting for similar comments.</li></ul></li></ul>","IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A big reasons why IBM’s market share has fallen in many different industries is because of their failure to invest in emerging technologies, which meant that other companies with began with much smaller market shares were able to out compete them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This demonstrates the importance of keeping up with technological innovation, as falling behind could mean losing a large part of your market share, especially in industries where it is easy to switch to a competitor.</p><h4>Notes</h4><ul><li>The industries that they lost in include: database software, operating systems, the PC market, semiconductors, etc.<ul><li>Much of its revenue comes from middle software and services (revenue growth has slowed here, however).</li><li>Its intense stock buyback program hurt growth in the 2010s.</li><li>Their investment in Linux was a good decision, since Linux has come to dominate the server industry, and their Red Hat serves as a main service that provides open source software to companies<ul><li>They adopt a flexible approach that doesn’t lock companies in when they choose to use their software, which they claim that customers wouldn’t want. <ul><li>This is interesting though, since they did something similar when they put DOS and OS2 on their computer, and it failed. This seems to have similar intentions.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage\">When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage</a><ul><li>The general claim that this case illustrates.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs\">Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs</a><ul><li>A structural version of the same failure, located in corporate form rather than in a single firm’s decisions.</li></ul></li></ul>","In order to recapture economic growth, the EU must deregulate and standardize market rules":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the past 50 years or so, Europe has been unable to recapture the explosive economic growth that characterized the post-war boom. This is because of the excessive regulation and fragmentation of the European Common Market.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates the negative impacts of regulation on economic growth, as many European regulations, such as the GDPR, despite the best intentions, have greatly reduced economic growth.</p><h4>Possible Reforms</h4><p>Currently, regulations are often conducted on a national basis, which introduces a barrier when trading across borders. This introduces a \"regulatory thicket\" whereby there are overlapping regulations and confusing laws, adding to the difficultly of international trade. Additionally, there is a lack of specialized commercial courts that slow down market enforcement. At the same time, the EU should rely on existing international organizations, as to not create duplicate organizations that only add bloat to the system. Lastly, regulation should be subject to greater rigorous cost-benefit analysis, as well as introducing sunset clauses, rather than having the regulations remain indefinitely.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The laws and regulations of the EU often end up spreading outside its borders\">The laws and regulations of the EU often end up spreading outside its borders</a></li></ul>","Japan's economy is sustained by a highly leveraged sovereign wealth fund":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Japan is able to sustain its economy through the central bank’s leveraging of the huge amount of savings in the country, whereby domestic savings are reinvested into higher-return, international assets.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The BOJ is able to enact their strategy due to the low interest rate in Japan, as well as the depreciated nature of the yen. However, if interest rates were to rise, or if the yen were to appreciate, this would likely lead to extremely, if any at all, margins. Ultimately, this system, though effective so far, puts the savings of ordinary Japanese in huge danger.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized\">The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Fukuyama, in <em>The End of History and the Last Man</em>, argued that with the end of the Cold War, we had reached the “end of history,” essentially that there would be nothing else that could challenge liberal democracy as the final evolution of ideology.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was a very influential idea which has been widely cited and critique in the coming decades.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>This is a very Hegelian idea (Hegelian Dialectic): thesis, antithesis, and synthesis.</li><li>Saw the end of the Cold War as evidence in support of modernization theory.</li><li>In this way, he was also opposed to the idea of political culture being the final form of political “war” to be fought.</li><li>He later argued that culture was still not a major force against the tide of global liberal democracy: - <strong>liberal democracy still had no serious ideological rival</strong>.<ul><li>Ex. Islam does not have a global appeal; it was only regionally powerful</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</span></li></ul>","Misaligned systems persist due to competition with misaligned incentives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the major causes of bad systems which, although they may operate very efficiently, maximize the wrong output, is that the competition inherent in the system is maximizing something that is not generally human-value aligned.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major cause of some bad systems extant in the world.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Within a system, there may be multiple equilibria such that when competition induces people to make processes more efficient, it merely incentives them to move to the most accessible equilibrium, which may not, in absolute terms, be the optimal outcome. The classic example of this is the prisoners dilemma and races to the bottom.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>I think that this claim may be too broad to be helpful, since some things in the world clearly are correctly aligned. What’s more, it’s not clear that human values are, in general, internally consistent. People may support higher wages for workers, for instance, and demand that the prices of basic necessities be held constant. Yet these two inclinations are not always coherent. Advocating for higher wages for minimum wage workers could make the prices of basic necessities increase, and so, we are never able to fully satisfy human values.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A viable system must be internally consistent\">A viable system must be internally consistent</a><ul><li>The issue is that, in many cases, the axioms that underlie a system are not internally consistent.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a><ul><li>This could be an example of a misaligned incentive.</li></ul></li></ul>","More Chinese AI firms might be founded in the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the arbitrary nature of the government and thus weaker protection for their rights, it is theorized that more Chinese AI firms might just be founded in the US.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This would allow the US to intake any AI expertise that is the product of the intense domestic AI competition in China.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Some factors, such as the fact that energy is more expensive could, however, be a big factors against starting firms in the US. Also, and ironically, this probably only applies to AI, i.e., firms that are related areas of national security since these are the only areas where the state might have an incentive to prevent M&amp;A with a foreign firm.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is a structural gap between Chinese and US AI capabilities\">There is a structural gap between Chinese and US AI capabilities</a><ul><li>This might be the reason that Chinese firms will be founded in the US in the future.</li></ul></li></ul>","People are a product of their times and circumstance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The way people are often is the result of the circumstances either familial or social that they were born into.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves both as an ethical question when it comes to assigning blame to such individuals when they commit wrongs that were normalized in the circumstances of their upbring, and also is a common theme in literature that heightens tragedy.</p><h4>Example</h4><p>In the film <em>Ran</em>, both the aging warlord and his sons are are the product of their times, since they all lived in the Sengoku Jidai period. As a result, the warlord is ruthless and bloodthirsty, and this eventually comes back to bite him when his sons go after him to secure their own power. Another example of this is in <em>All the King’s Men</em>. Stark, although he was originally someone who was innocent and good hearted, developed into the ruthless dictator that he later became through the influence of the world of politics.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some people must deserve something\">Some people must deserve something</a><ul><li>Even though people are shaped by their environment, and therefore lower their level of responsibility, they still must deserve something?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Aristotelian merit is virtue rather than endowment, and so escapes the natural contingency objection\">Aristotelian merit is virtue rather than endowment, and so escapes the natural contingency objection</a><ul><li>Aristotle would argue that despite one’s environment, one’s virtue is one’s own, and thus, a truly virtuous person would be able to overcome any such difficulties.</li></ul></li></ul>","Scope creep makes government services ineffective and inefficient":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Scope creep, or the gradual expansion of the goals of a project beyond its original scope, usually wrecks the efficiency and effectiveness of government services.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument for the privatization of certain government projects, or at least the checking of certain government projects to ensure that they actually carry our their original responsibilities rather than have the problem of scope creep.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>One major example of this is public transportation, especially in the US. While public transportation is intended to mitigate the fact that some low-income households do not own vehicles, due to scope creep, this does not often occur.</p><ul><li>The introduction of electric buses increases the cost of running the vehicles, meaning that less vehicles can be deployed, lowering ridership.<ul><li>The ironic thing is that it is very likely that emissions would have been lower if more investments had just been made such that routes were improved and ride frequencies were improved. </li></ul></li><li>Public transportation is extremely important for people to be able to have access to better schools and for them to be able to access better jobs.</li></ul><p>Ultimately, if the public transportation service just sought to provide the best service possible, they would have been able to tackle their main goal of increasing ridership far more successfully. However, due to scope creep, they move further away from achieving their original objectives.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“American transit is bad because of scope creep”</p><p>Every dollar spent complying with Buy America \\[(a law that requires the purchasing of American made products)] or staging community meetings is a dollar not spent <a href=\"https://worksinprogress.co/issue/the-united-states-needs-fewer-bus-stops/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">improving</a> frequency or coverage. Assuming the median cost per hour for operating a bus in the US is <a href=\"https://www.transit.dot.gov/ntd/data-product/ts21-service-data-and-operating-expenses-time-series-mode-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">$131</a>, and that the median US bus carries 14.3 passengers per hour, <strong>the money for electrifying one bus could fund roughly 2,300 additional service hours, carrying at least 30,000 additional riders.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>Related idea about the entrenched nature of the government and why it is difficult to solve the problem of scope creep once it happens.</li></ul></li></ul>","Select into office the person with the greatest amount of the trait required and which is the binding constraint":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When it comes to deciding how to pick people to fill out a government position, it should be based on selecting the person who has the greatest amount of some skill relevant to the position and which represents a binding constraint.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is helpful heuristic that Aristotle provides for guiding people to put the best people into office.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle gives the example that, for instance, if we are selecting someone to be the manager of the treasury, we should select the person with the most honesty, since honesty is the binding constraint. There are other things required for the job, such has knowledge of finance or general wisdom, but the binding constraint is the most important factor, since its the rarest.</p><h4>References</h4>","Since the Industrial Revolution, inventions have occurred more readily":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since the industrial revolution, inventions have appeared at a rate that is closer to the theoretical maximum.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It is unclear whether this is causation or merely correlation, but it is nonetheless important to note the increase in the rate of innovation with the Industrial Revolution.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> It seems likelier that the Industrial Revolution was the <em>cause</em> of the shortening time between the theoretical earliest possible time of invention and the actual time of invention than that there was merely a correlation. This is due to the fact that the industrialization created many new tools and materials that were able to make tools, leading both the ability to mass produce an invention and in case also the ability to create the invention in the first place.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge\">Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge</a><ul><li>These two notes seen to be highly correlated, and part of the reason why it seems that the Industrial Revolution was the <em>cause</em> of the increased innovation.</li></ul></li></ul>","The LSRL always goes through the mean x and y values":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The least-squares regression line always goes through the means of the x and y values ($\\bar{x},\\bar{y}$).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a property of the LSRL, which may be useful for an AP Stats test.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The lower the variability around the regression line, the higher the coefficient of determination\">The lower the variability around the regression line, the higher the coefficient of determination</a></li></ul>","The Protestant Work Ethics is a work that sought to refute the claims of Economic Determinists":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Marx’s original claim of the economic substructure that underlaid the cultural superstructure is refuted (intentional or not) by Weber’s work in the Protestant Work Ethic.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves as a refutation by Weber of Marx’s strict conception of economic determinism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In essence, although many of Weber’s claims are spurious, he at least showed that a non-trivial amount of influence is exerted on the economic substructure by the cultural superstructure. This undermines Marx’s claims.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that if Weber’s claims are even in the slightest true, then the entire chain of causation, either forwards or backwards between culture and economics, is broken. Therefore, it seems to be to invalidate conceptualizing society by simplifying its causal factors to these two.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Frankfurt School shaped postmodernism and modern progressive activism\">The Frankfurt School shaped postmodernism and modern progressive activism</a><ul><li>One of the major innovations of the Frankfurt school is their acknowledgment of the influence of culture on economics.</li></ul></li></ul>","The state should be entirely in charge of education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The state should entirely be in charge of education both because it is important for the city to be able to cultivate virtue (and people must have the common goal and an identical vision of virtue).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the constitution of the ideal city, since in the ideal city, everyone cultivate virtue and they all have the same goal of virtue.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle views education as a crucial part to creating a good city, and thus, in order to facilitate the best education, the state must be thus in charge of the education and educate all citizens such that they would be able to cultivate virtue. For Aristotle, this education, very roughly, includes:</p><ul><li>Light physical education up until puberty.</li><li>During the three years after puberty, they engage more intensely to other subjects of learning (language, art, etc.)</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>It seems that in this way, Aristotle is very similar.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a><ul><li>Mill would disagree with this, for the fear that it causes citizens to be too similar, and thus, the society loses out on the benefits of greater individuality.</li></ul></li></ul>","The traditional left-right split is turning into a split between the well and the poorly informed":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The traditional dichotomy between the left and the right is slowly becoming a split between the people who are well informed and those who are ill-informed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a unique diagnosis of the current trend of polarization between the left and the right.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Only Democrats engaged with serious publications in substantial numbers, such as the New York Times and the Washington Post, while Republicans mainly rely on watching Fox News for information. The people who are more poorly informed (Republicans) are drifting towards authoritarianism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets\">AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets</a><ul><li>Does AI have the power to relieve the information asymmetry in general, and make people generally better informed?</li></ul></li></ul>","We ought not to interference with the personal liberties of other individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We should not interfere with the personal liberties of others, for a variety of reasons.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core axiom of liberalism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are many explanations which can be given as to why this is ought to be followed.</p><ul><li>We don’t necessarily know what is best for others.</li><li>It could be that people who act unorthodoxly are actually right, and by suppressing their behavior, we are in fact moving further away from the truth.</li></ul><p>We cannot justify repression of individual via the fact that:</p><ul><li><em>The person is harming themselves in the absence of outside intervention.</em> <ul><li>But the wider society rarely knows about what is good or bad for the person, and may, in an attempt to prevent “bad” behavior, wipe out a potentially good thing.</li><li>For something like not taking care of one self’s appearance, though this may affect the general interest in some minute way, we must accept this in order to preserve general person liberties.</li></ul></li><li><em>The person is setting a bad example for others.</em><ul><li>If its the case that the person is setting a bad example for others, there would naturally be bad consequences for others to see, and thus, others would avoid that person’s behavior.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>There are some personal injuries which must be tolerated, since they arise out of people considering the protection of their own rights and liberties.</p><ul><li>For instance, someone who is short tempered may have very few friends, though they cannot claim that this is an injustice done to them, since people are merely protecting their own interests.</li><li>Likewise, societal institutions such as competition cannot cause harm, since there has not been an unwarranted invasion of one’s rights which is contrary to the general interest.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>This is one instance where the rule laid out in this note can be vioalted.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>Another case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society\">We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society</a><ul><li>Another related idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Yang of Sui's excessive spending, disregard for human life, and general poor management led to the collapse of the Sui":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Emperor' Yang’s poor management, including his reckless invasions of Goguryeo and Champa, as well as his efforts to complete the Grand Canal and Great Wall led to his downfall and the end of the Sui.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows how overspending and poor management can lead to the downfall of an empire, even when before, the country is stable and prosperous.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion\">When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion</a><ul><li>This is the principle which this case serves the demonstrate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill\">Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill</a><ul><li>Another similar case study, except revolution and overthrow was avoided.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
